Patch 1003: "JOBCRT Ver14" jobs get the job context's low 28 bits too (J-Stars had no sound at all).

The job context word at +0x14 is sizeJobDescriptor:4 plus 28 bits that v0.18.0 fills only for a "JOBCRT Ver13" crt. J-Stars'
SPU jobs carry "JOBCRT Ver14" (CRI's AtomDecoder and AsrVoice sound jobs and the game's own jobs). That crt checks the same bits
before main and STOPs with 0x3FFF when they are 0, so the sound decoder never ran and every cellAudio block was silent. Ver14 jobs
now get the LS address of their descriptor there, as jm2 leaves it (the fix the v0.15 port made, SPURS_CTX_PAD); the game's jobs
ignore the field. SPURS_CTX_PAD=0 turns it off for an A/B.

Tested (US disc, title and menus, 240 s each): before 100.0% silent blocks in every window; with it 0.0% silent once the sound
starts; with SPURS_CTX_PAD=0 silent again.

diff --git a/runtime/spu/spurs_job.c b/runtime/spu/spurs_job.c
--- a/runtime/spu/spurs_job.c
+++ b/runtime/spu/spurs_job.c
@@ -274,8 +274,18 @@
         ls32(ls, 0x1CC, JOB_DMA_TAG);
         memset(ls + 0x210, 0, 0x10);
     }
+    /* A "JOBCRT Ver14" crt (J-Stars: CRI's AtomDecoder and AsrVoice sound
+     * jobs, and the game's own jobs) checks the same low 28 bits before main
+     * and STOPs with 0x3FFF when they are 0: the sound decoder never ran and
+     * the game was silent. Give those jobs the LS address of their descriptor,
+     * as jm2 leaves it; the game's jobs ignore the field. SPURS_CTX_PAD=0 turns
+     * this off (A/B). */
+    uint32_t crt14_pad = 0;
+    { static int pad = -1;
+      if (pad < 0) { const char* e = getenv("SPURS_CTX_PAD"); pad = (e && *e == '0') ? 0 : 1; }
+      if (pad && !jm_iface && memcmp(ls + 0x30, "JOBCRT Ver14", 12) == 0) crt14_pad = desc_ls & 0x0FFFFFFFu; }
     ls32(ls, ctx_ls + JC_SIZE_JOB_DESC,
-         ((dsz == 64 ? 0u : (dsz / 128u) & 0xFu) << 28) | jm_iface);
+         ((dsz == 64 ? 0u : (dsz / 128u) & 0xFu) << 28) | jm_iface | crt14_pad);
     ls16(ls, ctx_ls + JC_NUM_IO_BUFFER,    (uint16_t)n_dma);
     ls16(ls, ctx_ls + JC_NUM_CACHE_BUFFER, (uint16_t)n_cache);
     ls32(ls, ctx_ls + JC_O_BUFFER, out_ls);
